<p>Port Arthur flood damage claims usually start in the worst conditions: wet drywall, ruined flooring, and a house that smells of river water within a day. Flood insurance is its own product with its own rules, and many owners learn the details only after the water goes down. This page covers how flood claims work under the National Flood Insurance Program (NFIP) and private flood policies, how flood differs from wind and plumbing losses, and where these claims tend to go wrong.</p>

<h2>Flooding Risk in Port Arthur and Jefferson County</h2>
<p>Port Arthur faces flood risk from several directions. The city’s hurricane flood protection levee and seawall system reduces the risk of storm surge from Sabine Lake, but it does not stop flooding from extreme rainfall that cannot drain fast enough. Harvey proved that in 2017. The <a href="https://www.weather.gov/lch/2017Harvey" target="_blank" rel="noopener">National Weather Service Lake Charles</a> reports storm totals over 40 inches across the area, a record 60.58 inches near Nederland, and more than 64,000 homes flooded in Jefferson County, with Port Arthur and Groves among the hardest hit. Two years later, Tropical Storm Imelda brought preliminary rainfall totals of more than 40 inches to parts of the county and widespread flash flooding.</p>
<p>Surge remains a threat outside the levee and along the bayous. During Ike in 2008, NOAA records a 14–15 foot surge at Sabine Pass and widespread flooding where the surge backed up Hillebrandt and Taylor bayous (<a href="https://www.ncdc.noaa.gov/stormevents/eventdetails.jsp?id=136895" target="_blank" rel="noopener">NCEI event record</a>). Our <a href="/storm-history/">storm history</a> page covers these events in more detail.</p>

<h2>Flood vs. Wind vs. Water Damage</h2>
<p>The policy that pays depends on where the water came from. This is the single biggest issue in Southeast Texas water claims.</p>
