<h2>How an NFIP Flood Claim Works</h2>
<p>NFIP policies are written on the federal Standard Flood Insurance Policy and handled by private insurers or NFIP Direct. The broad steps, based on <a href="https://www.fema.gov/fact-sheet/file-your-national-flood-insurance-policy-claim" target="_blank" rel="noopener">FEMA guidance</a> and the <a href="https://www.floodsmart.gov/claims-checklist" target="_blank" rel="noopener">FloodSmart claims checklist</a>, are:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Report the loss</strong> to your flood insurer as soon as possible.</li>
<li><strong>Document before you discard.</strong> Photograph damaged items, water lines, and each room. Keep swatches of carpet and samples of flooring when possible.</li>
<li><strong>Dry out the home.</strong> Remove wet drywall, insulation, and flooring as needed to prevent mold. Keep receipts for cleanup supplies and equipment.</li>
<li><strong>Meet the adjuster</strong> and get the adjuster’s contact information and your claim number.</li>
<li><strong>Submit a signed proof of loss</strong> with supporting documents. FEMA says it is generally due within 60 days of the loss, even if you disagree with the adjuster’s estimate. FEMA can extend this deadline after some disasters, but you should not assume it will.</li>
<li><strong>Request additional payment</strong> if more damage is found later, with documentation.</li>
</ol>

[[cta]]

<h2>Common Problems in Flood Claims</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Missed proof of loss.</strong> Owners wait for the adjuster’s estimate and miss the 60-day window.</li>
<li><strong>Waiting period.</strong> NFIP policies generally have a 30-day waiting period, so a policy bought as a storm approaches usually will not cover it.</li>
<li><strong>Pier-and-beam and crawlspace limits.</strong> Many older Port Arthur homes sit on piers. NFIP limits coverage for items in areas below the lowest elevated floor, which affects ductwork, insulation, and equipment under the house.</li>
<li><strong>Contents not covered.</strong> Contents coverage is purchased separately. Some owners discover they insured only the building.</li>
<li><strong>Underpriced scopes.</strong> Estimates that dry and reinstall rather than replace, or that cut drywall too low on the wall.</li>
<li><strong>Prior flood history.</strong> Homes flooded in Harvey and again in Imelda may face questions about whether earlier damage was repaired.</li>
</ul>
<p>Private flood policies can differ from the NFIP on limits, waiting periods, additional living expenses, and deadlines. Read the specific policy.</p>

<h2>Rebuilding After a Flood in Port Arthur</h2>
<p>Substantially damaged homes may have to meet floodplain and building requirements when they are repaired. The City of Port Arthur Building Inspections Division enforces the 2021 International Residential Code and requires permits for structural work. Confirm your scope with the city before rebuilding, and see our <a href="/local-building-codes/">local building codes</a> page. Code compliance costs are not automatically paid by every policy, so review your coverage.</p>

<h2>How a Port Arthur Public Adjuster Can Help With Flood Claims</h2>
<p>A public adjuster works for the policyholder. On a flood claim, that may include measuring and photographing every room, recording moisture readings, preparing a building estimate and a contents inventory, tracking proof of loss deadlines, and coordinating with a separate wind or homeowners claim when both apply. Port Arthur Public Adjuster serves Port Arthur, Jefferson County, and surrounding Southeast Texas communities, including <a href="/service-areas/groves/">Groves</a>, Nederland, Beaumont, and <a href="/service-areas/orange/">Orange</a>. We cannot guarantee any outcome. Fees are disclosed in a written contract before any work begins; Texas law caps public adjuster compensation at 10% of the insurance settlement.</p>
<p>If you are dealing with a hurricane that caused both wind and flood damage, start with our <a href="/services/hurricane-damage-claims/">hurricane damage claims</a> page.</p>
